I have the full `_capResumedContext`. Analysis of the new `else` branch:

The structure:
```dart
if (!shouldCompact(...) && !overCap) {
 return; // under trigger: nothing to do
}
if (tokens > _effectiveContextWindow) {
 // local-trim valve
 if (trimmed != null) { ...; return; }
 // fall through to LM pass (last resort)
} else {
 // NEW: over trigger, inside window → defer
 _logDiagnostic(...);
 return;
}
_pushBusyPhase('Compacting context…');
...LM pass...
``

Wait — there's an important subtlety. The `else` branch catches `!shouldCompact && overCap` (message-count trigger, tokens under window) AND `shouldCompact && tokens <= window`... wait can shouldCompact be true while tokens <= window? shouldCompact(tokens, window, settings) — the trigger is presumably tokens > window - reserve. So "over trigger" means tokens > window − reserve, which can still be ≤ window. So both regimes land in the else branch: (a) overCap message-count gate with low tokens, (b) token-trigger over but under window. Both defer. Good.

Now the deferred case: overCap (message-count gate) — `_messageCapGateReached()` includes `takePremptivePass(model.provider, model.id)` — a ONE-SHOT E1 pass armed by a cap-unknown refusal. Note: `_messageCapGateReached()` CONSUMES the one-shot pass (`takePremptivePass` — "take" implies it consumes it). So the boot calls `_messageCapGateReached()`, which consumes the one-shot preemptive pass, then defers! At the first turn, `_maybeAutoCompact` calls `_messageCapGateReached()` again — the one-shot was already consumed at boot, so `overCap` is false at the turn... unless the token trigger also trips.

Hold on, let me think about this carefully. The E1 one-shot pass: a cap-unknown refusal arms a one-shot preemptive pass. `_messageCapGateReached()` returns true and CONSUMES it. In the OLD code (before this PR), the boot cap would then run the compaction pass immediately — consistent with the consumption. In the NEW code, the boot consumes the one-shot pass and then defers to the first turn's pre-flight. At the first turn, `_maybeAutoCompact` gates on `shouldCompact(tokens, ...) || _messageCapGateReached()`. If tokens are under the token-trigger AND the one-shot was consumed at boot, the pre-flight does NOT compact. The message-count condition (messageCapTriggerReached — messages >= cap − margin) might still hold, but if learnedCapFor returns null (cap unknown — that's the case where the one-shot was armed), messageCapTriggerReached returns false.

So: boot consumes the one-shot E1 pass and defers; the first turn's pre-flight no longer sees the gate → sends the request → the provider refuses again (cap unknown) → re-arms another one-shot pass? The PR #1695 comment says the gate "consumes the one-shot E1 pass a cap-unknown refusal armed (consumed here so it fires exactly once per refusal)". So each refusal arms one pass; if the boot consumes it and defers it into nothing, the user pays one extra refusal round-trip. Hmm, but wait — in the old code, the boot cap ran it immediately, so the pass was used at boot. In the new code it's consumed and DROPPED (defered to a pre-flight that won't fire).

Actually wait — will the pre-flight not fire? `_messageCapGateReached()` at turn: `messageCapTriggerReached(...)` — cap unknown → false. `takePremptivePass(...)` — already consumed at boot → false. So the gate is false at the first turn. `shouldCompact(tokens, window)` — if tokens under trigger → false. So no compaction at first turn. The request goes out with the message count over the learned... wait no, cap is UNKNOWN in this scenario (cap-unknown refusal armed the one-shot). Hmm, actually the scenario per PR #1695: a model with a learned cap? Let me re-read: "the additive message-count trigger — a model with a learned cap compacts when the live count reaches `cap − margin`, BEFORE the request, even when the token trigger is untouched. The gate also consumes the one-shot E1 pass a cap-unknown refusal armed (PR #1695)."

So two sub-cases:
