I have the full `_capResumedContext`. Analysis of the new `else` branch:

The structure:
```dart
if (!shouldCompact(...) && !overCap) {
 return; // under trigger: nothing to do
}
if (tokens > _effectiveContextWindow) {
 // local-trim valve
 if (trimmed != null) { ...; return; }
 // fall through to LM pass (last resort)
} else {
 // NEW: over trigger, inside window → defer
 _logDiagnostic(...);
 return;
}
_pushBusyPhase('Compacting context…');
...LM pass...
``

Wait — there's an important subtlety. The `else` branch catches `!shouldCompact && overCap` (message-count trigger, tokens under window) AND `shouldCompact && tokens <= window`... wait can shouldCompact be true while tokens <= window? shouldCompact(tokens, window, settings) — the trigger is presumably tokens > window - reserve. So "over trigger" means tokens > window − reserve, which can still be ≤ window. So both regimes land in the else branch: (a) overCap message-count gate with low tokens, (b) token-trigger over but under window. Both defer. Good.

Now the deferred case: overCap (message-count gate) — `_messageCapGateReached()` includes `takePremptivePass(model.provider, model.id)` — a ONE-SHOT E1 pass armed by a cap-unknown refusal. Note: `_messageCapGateReached()` CONSUMES the one-shot pass (`takePremptivePass` — "take" implies it consumes it). So the boot calls `_messageCapGateReached()`, which consumes the one-shot preemptive pass, then defers! At the first turn, `_maybeAutoCompact` calls `_messageCapGateReached()` again — the one-shot was already consumed at boot, so `overCap` is false at the turn... unless the token trigger also trips.

